---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy — your images never leave your device"
  description="How these image tools protect your privacy: local browser processing, no uploads, no filenames or metadata transmitted, and what limited site-level data exists."
  path="/privacy"
>
  <div class="container section">
    <div class="container container--narrow" style="padding-inline: 0;">
      <div class="prose">
        <h1>Privacy</h1>
        <p><strong>Your images never leave your device.</strong> This is not a policy promise — it is the architecture.</p>

        <h2>How image processing works</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>You select or drop a file; your browser reads it locally.</li>
          <li>Decoding, resizing, compressing, cropping and re-encoding all run inside the page using browser APIs.</li>
          <li>The result is produced as a local download. There is no upload step at any point.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          You can verify this yourself: open your browser's network inspector while using any tool.
          Aside from loading the site's own static files, no requests carrying your image occur.
        </p>

        <h2>What we deliberately never transmit</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Image bytes or pixel content</li>
          <li>File names</li>
          <li>EXIF, GPS or any other metadata values</li>
          <li>Processing results or download activity</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What the site itself collects</h2>
        <p>
          This build ships with <strong>no third-party analytics, no advertising scripts and no
          external trackers</strong>. Fonts and scripts are served from this site only. If
          privacy-conscious, aggregate tool events are added later (for example "compression
          completed"), they will be limited to non-identifying categories — never filenames,
          image content or metadata — and this page will be updated before they go live.
        </p>

        <h2>Local storage</h2>
        <p>
          Tools may remember interface preferences (such as your last-used quality mode) in your
          browser's local storage. That data stays in your browser and can be cleared at any time
          via your browser settings.
        </p>

        <h2>Third parties</h2>
        <p>
          Platform names used in presets (Instagram, YouTube, LinkedIn, etc.) refer to trademark
          owners of those platforms. No data is shared with them by using this site; the presets
          are simply published dimensions.
        </p>

        <h2>Your rights and questions</h2>
        <p>
          Because no personal data is received by this site, there is generally nothing for us to
          delete or export on your behalf. If that ever changes, this page will state it plainly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
